#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120724} :activity/id #uuid "e15d96c3-59f3-5ea3-8c30-d6c68ac605ad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121054 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ that is given by the equation \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 2 , 3 , 1 \\rangle ) \\cdot \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, note that $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle$ is a vector that's normal to the plane.\n\n![](images/7b/7b2dfcd87c1f5e4e2c2b1a0b9a7f73899772e33e1020465b2b38d1b2de29a66c.png)\n\nLet's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,3,1)$. Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,1⟩-⟨3,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26e494eef47db4dba3ac36dfa96c7e9a9d191321b22bb82874f3e74bbd7116ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+12+6|}{\\sqrt{16+16+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is $\\dfrac{7}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "224c4104-9097-5a92-9c37-5d0fe22171a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3261 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9e02b271-c1ce-518a-b549-4e83a2061ea7" :step/math-academy-id 17940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 175921